        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 2, "high": 4, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.20,
      "why": "Shared datapath with k rotations/cycle uses far fewer FFs than the pipelined families (only a small FSM + a few registers). It can plausibly hit >=1 MSPS if Fmax x k >= 1, making it a strong low-area candidate. k=2..4 keeps the per-cycle critical path manageable. This is the main competitor for the min-luts_plus_ffs selection rule, so it must be explored to map the true low-area end of the front."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec is area-dominated (min luts_plus_ffs) with a hard throughput floor of 1 MSPS and a hard accuracy floor of 2^-10. The iterative family is excluded: at 1 result/cycle it cannot reach 1 MSPS on Artix-7, so it cannot satisfy the throughput constraint. That leaves the three 1/cycle-capable families. pipelined_m is the primary area/throughput trade-off (FFs vs Fmax), pipelined is the high-Fmax anchor, and unrolled_k is the low-FF challenger. I bound data_width to 12..18 and n_iter to 10..16 because accuracy depends only on these plus the guards: W=12 already gives an output LSB of 2^-10, and N=10..16 with small guards comfortably meets err <= 2^-10 while leaving room to push accuracy_bits above the HV reference of 10. Guards are kept small (0..2) since area is the objective and large guards only add LUTs/FFs for accuracy we do not need. Rounding is left to both choices so the search can pick the cheaper trunc where it still meets the error bound. Budget is weighted toward pipelined_m (45%) as the expected winner, with pipelined (35%) and unrolled_k (20%) to map the rest of the front."
}

